---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/hgb/drs24.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  framework="hgb"
  status={metadata.status}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Scope" },        { id: "s2", label: "Purchase price allocation" },        { id: "s3", label: "Worked example" },        { id: "s4", label: "IFRS" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Scope — a Shift From General Treatment to Group-Specific Focus</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card info span2">
<div class="ctitle">Genuinely narrower and deeper than its predecessor</div>
<div class="principle">Where DRS 12 (withdrawn, covered earlier) addressed intangible fixed assets generally, DRS 24 — part of the same 23 February 2016 coordinated release as DRS 22 and DRS 23 — focuses specifically on the CONSOLIDATION-specific dimension: identifying and measuring intangible assets that emerge or become separately recognisable ONLY as part of a business acquisition, building directly on §301's own capital consolidation mechanics established in the earlier HGB poster series.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Purchase Price Allocation — Identifying What §246 Alone Might Miss</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="cols-1">
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">Beyond the acquiree's own balance sheet</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 24 requires a genuine purchase price allocation exercise identifying intangible assets that may NEVER have appeared on the ACQUIRED company's own individual balance sheet — customer relationships, brand names, order backlogs, non-contractual customer lists, and similar items — which, though the acquiree itself may have been prohibited from capitalising internally-generated versions under §248(2)'s own exclusions (established in the very early HGB poster series), become SEPARATELY IDENTIFIABLE and recognisable once acquired as part of a business combination.</div>
</div>
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">Recognition criteria DRS 24 elaborates</div>
<div class="check-list">
<span>Separability from goodwill — can the asset genuinely be sold, transferred, or licensed independently?</span>
<span>Contractual/legal basis — does the asset arise from contractual or other legal rights?</span>
<span>Reliable measurability — can a defensible fair value genuinely be determined?</span>
</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Worked Example — Recognising a Customer Relationship on Acquisition</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="worked">
<div class="wk-title">An intangible asset that never existed on the acquiree's own books</div>
<div class="wk-body">A group acquires a company with a genuinely valuable, long-standing customer base — built up over decades through the acquired company's own internal marketing and relationship-building efforts, and therefore NEVER capitalised on its individual balance sheet under §248(2)'s own restrictions. An independent valuation attributes €1,200,000 of the total purchase price specifically to this customer relationship.</div>
<div class="wk-answer">Under DRS 24's purchase price allocation guidance, the group recognises a SEPARATE €1,200,000 "customer relationships" intangible asset in the Konzernbilanz, distinct from goodwill — REDUCING the residual goodwill that would otherwise have absorbed this value, and subjecting the €1,200,000 to its OWN mandatory amortisation under §246(1) sentence 4/§309(1), rather than remaining buried within an undifferentiated goodwill figure.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 3)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="ctitle">Strong methodological convergence, differing amortisation destiny</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 24's purchase price allocation methodology closely parallels IFRS 3's own approach to identifying separately-recognisable intangible assets on acquisition — genuinely strong convergence in the IDENTIFICATION and INITIAL MEASUREMENT process. The recognised intangibles' own SUBSEQUENT treatment then diverges along the SAME familiar line: HGB requires ongoing amortisation for these intangibles too (useful life permitting), while IFRS applies impairment-only testing for indefinite-life intangibles specifically, though FINITE-life intangibles are amortised under both frameworks.</div>
</div>
</div>
</StandardPageLayout>
